## The Mirror of Vastness

Yet the overview effect was not a universal solvent for human limitation. The multiplanetary man had read the darker testimonies too, and they troubled him more than the transcendent ones because they revealed what the effect actually was: not a cure, but a catalyst. A mirror that showed the viewer what the viewer brought to the viewing.

Buzz Aldrin had walked on the Moon, achieved the ultimate human goal before the age of forty, and then descended into a spiral of clinical depression and alcoholism that consumed decades of his life. His marriage to his childhood sweetheart broke up. He remarried in haste and was divorced again within two years. He ended up working at a Cadillac dealership in Beverly Hills and failed to sell a single vehicle in six months. The overview effect had not saved him. It had broken him—or more precisely, it had revealed the fractures that were already there, magnifying them against a cosmic background that offered no comfort and no structure to a mind that had lost its terrestrial moorings.

The research confirmed what his intuition had already assembled. A comprehensive thesis from the University of Ottawa examining how people react to cosmic vastness found that it "can evoke polarizing existential experiences." While most participants reported positive experiences—93 percent in one study—a substantial majority also reported negative experiences at 68 percent, including existential distress at 46 percent. The polarization was genuine: when exposed to cosmic vastness, approximately half of participants reported more elevating experience, and half reported more existential distress. The difference was not random. Self-esteem moderated the relationship between witnessing cosmic vastness and elevating experience, while both self-esteem and meaning in life moderated the relationship between cosmic vastness and existential distress.

The finding had been replicated independently. A study published in the *Journal of Experimental Social Psychology*, with the evocative title "A microscopic dot on a microscopic dot," demonstrated that "priming cosmic enormity makes low self-esteem people feel less oneness with others" and that low self-esteem people exposed to the vastness of the universe "reported more negative affect, less identification with others, less empathy to victims of a humanitarian disaster, and lower egalitarianism". For high self-esteem individuals, no such effects emerged. The universe did not speak with a single voice. It amplified whatever signal the psyche was already broadcasting.

William Shatner, the actor who had spent decades pretending to explore space as Captain Kirk, experienced something closer to Aldrin's descent than Mitchell's transcendence when he finally traveled to the edge of space aboard Blue Origin in October 2021. "My trip to space was supposed to be a celebration; instead, it felt like a funeral," he wrote. "It was among the strongest feelings of grief I have ever encountered. The contrast between the vicious coldness of space and the warm nurturing of Earth below filled me with overwhelming sadness". Shatner saw not unity but death—"the writhing, slow death of Earth and we on it." He wept for the planet because he realized it was dying, and the political systems that might have intervened were consumed by conflicts that looked, from altitude, like children fighting in a burning house.

The multiplanetary man processed these findings with the clinical precision he had developed over decades of psychological self-examination. The overview effect was a stress test for psychological infrastructure. A mind that had done the preparatory work—the dismantling of tribal identity, the cultivation of emotional regulation, the expansion of temporal perspective, the construction of a meaning framework large enough to accommodate cosmic scale—would experience the overview effect as a completion, a capstone that integrated years of inner work into a single, coherent vision. A mind that had not done that work would experience the same view as threat, as diminishment, as an assault on the significance of the self.

But the research also introduced a complication that the celebratory literature had largely ignored. A 2026 paper by Liu, Gu, and Fu in *The Innovation Informatics* reframed the overview effect not merely as a moment of inspiration but as a psychophysiological event unfolding within the wider ecology of spaceflight stress. The authors argued that as missions extend in duration and crews become more heterogeneous, the overview effect can no longer be treated as a reliably positive experience. It may support resilience, they wrote, but it may also become a challenge—a source of psychological strain that requires active management across mission stages. A 2026 working paper on long-duration spaceflight went further, noting the "documented decay of the Overview Effect on long missions—the point at which a receding Earth shifts from a source of awe to a source of distress". The transformation, in other words, was not guaranteed to last. The awe could curdle. The connection could invert into longing, and the longing into grief.

The multiplanetary man had felt this himself, though he had not yet named it. The overview effect had given him the planet. But the same view that revealed the planet's beauty had also revealed the void that surrounded it. And the void did not fade with familiarity. It deepened.

