## The Fragility of the Transformation

The question that occupied the months and years following his return was not whether the experience had changed him—it clearly had—but whether the change would prove durable. The research on the overview effect had demonstrated its power to shift perspective in the moment. But the psychological literature on awe and prosocial behavior suggested that the effects, while real, could fade. The small self induced by vastness could contract again. The expanded identity could shrink back to its previous boundaries. The dissolution of borders could be reversed by the daily friction of terrestrial life. *The Atlantic* had noted, with a bluntness that the celebratory accounts rarely matched, that "as powerful as it can be, the overview effect fades. Eventually, gravity and worldly responsibilities restore their hold".

The multiplanetary man was determined that this would not happen to him. He had spent too many decades building the psychological infrastructure for this transformation to allow it to dissipate into memory. He instituted practices of deliberate recollection—not the casual reminiscence of a pleasant experience, but the disciplined re-evocation of the view, summoning the image of Earth from orbit with the same intentionality he had once applied to emotional regulation and temporal anchoring. He had learned, in his earlier psychological work, that the mind could be trained to hold states that it could not initially sustain. The overview effect would be no different.

The research supported his intuition, with qualifications. Studies had demonstrated that human-nature connectedness—the sense of oneness with the natural world—was a reliable predictor of both psychological well-being and pro-environmental behavior. A 2024 pilot study found that virtual reality inductions of the overview effect could significantly increase human-nature connectedness scores compared to control conditions. The shift was not merely emotional; it was measurable, and it could be reinforced through repetition. But the same research that showed the effect could be induced also showed that the dose mattered. A seven-minute video produced no measurable change. A fifteen-minute immersive experience produced suggestive but not statistically significant effects. The transformation was real, but it was fragile. It required maintenance.

The evidence from his own life suggested that the maintenance was possible. Months after his return, he noticed that certain cognitive patterns had not reverted. When he read news of international conflicts, the tribal framing that had once seemed natural now felt alien—not merely wrong, but structurally incomprehensible, like watching people argue over property lines drawn on a map that had been placed over a photograph of the entire planet. When he encountered political rhetoric that treated national interest as the ultimate horizon of moral concern, he experienced a visceral dissonance that was not intellectual disagreement but something closer to perceptual error—the feeling one has when looking at an optical illusion that the brain cannot resolve. He had seen the planet from the outside. The image had burned itself into his neural circuitry. He could not unsee it. He could not return to the tribal perspective that treated Earth as a background condition rather than a fragile, singular, irreplaceable home.

