## Planetary Empathy as Permanent Baseline

This was the mature form of his transformation: not the ecstatic transcendence of Mitchell nor the depressive collapse of Aldrin, but something quieter, more sustainable, and ultimately more useful. A permanent shift in the baseline of his moral concern.

The overview effect had given him a home—not a nation, not a culture, not an ideology, but the planet itself, the thin blue line of atmosphere, the iridescent biosphere that Ron Garan had seen teeming with life. He carried that home with him now, internalized, accessible at will. He did not need to be in orbit to access the perspective. He had become the perspective.

The research on commitment to the environment provided a framework for understanding this transformation. Studies by Davis, Green, and Reed demonstrated that "higher levels of commitment to the environment and greater inclusion of nature in the self separately predicted higher levels of pro-environmental behavior, even when controlling for social desirability and ecological worldview." The overview effect, by expanding the self to include the planet, had produced precisely this inclusion. Nature was no longer an external resource to be managed. It was part of the self. Harm to it registered as harm to his own being.

The practical consequences were significant. Decisions that had once required complex ethical reasoning—whether to prioritize short-term economic gain over long-term ecological stability, whether to externalize costs onto future generations, whether to treat national interests as paramount over planetary ones—became simpler. Not easier, but clearer. The overview effect had reorganized his values at a level below conscious deliberation. He did not need to argue himself into planetary stewardship. He had seen the planet from the outside, and the image had burned itself into his neural circuitry. He could not unsee it. He could not return to the tribal perspective that treated Earth as a background condition rather than a fragile, singular, irreplaceable home.

