## The Neuroscience of Terror

The research on what psychologists call "threat-based awe" had been accumulating for years, though it had received far less attention than the celebratory literature on awe's benefits. A 2017 study published in the *Journal of Personality and Social Psychology*, led by Amie Gordon and Dacher Keltner at Berkeley, tested whether there was a darker variant of awe that arose in response to vast, complex stimuli that were threatening. The researchers found that people experienced this type of awe with regularity—in response to tornadoes, terrorist attacks, wrathful gods—and that it differed from positive awe in every measurable dimension. Threat-based awe was appraised as lower in self-control and certainty, and higher in situational control. It was characterized by greater feelings of fear. And, crucially, it produced a different physiological signature: threat-based awe was associated with increased sympathetic autonomic arousal—the fight-or-flight response, the racing heart, the tightened chest, the body preparing for danger—whereas positive awe was associated with increased parasympathetic arousal, the calming, restorative branch of the nervous system.

The distinction mattered because it explained something the multiplanetary man had noticed but could not articulate. When he had first seen Earth from orbit, the experience had been physiologically calming—a deep, parasympathetic sense of connection and rightness. But when his attention had shifted from the planet to the space around it, the physiology had inverted. His heart rate had increased. His breathing had shortened. His muscles had tensed. His body had interpreted the infinite blackness not as a source of wonder but as a threat. And the research suggested that this was not a personal failure of preparation. It was a predictable response to a stimulus that combined vastness with danger—and the void, whatever else it was, was dangerous. It was the environment in which the thin blue line could be extinguished. It was the condition that made the planet's fragility not merely a fact to be contemplated but a vulnerability to be feared.

The 2017 study had also found that threat-based awe was mediated by feelings of powerlessness. The vastness of the threat overwhelmed the sense of agency. The self, confronted with something so large and so indifferent that no action could alter its fundamental character, experienced its own smallness not as a liberating expansion but as a diminishment. Positive awe produced what researchers called the "small self"—a diminished sense of personal importance that felt paradoxically liberating, because the self was small in relation to something meaningful and connective. Threat-based awe produced a different kind of smallness: a smallness in relation to something meaningless and indifferent, a smallness that felt like erasure rather than expansion.

The multiplanetary man recognized the difference immediately. When he had looked at Earth from orbit, his smallness had felt like belonging—he was part of a vast, interconnected system, and his individual concerns were relativized in a way that freed him from their grip. When he looked at the void, his smallness felt like abandonment. He was not part of the void. He was an accidental flicker of warmth in a cosmos that had no temperature, no preference, no awareness of his existence. The same scale that had produced planetary empathy was now producing something closer to cosmic vertigo.

