## The Polarization of Vastness

The research on how people respond to cosmic vastness had identified a pattern that the multiplanetary man had lived but had not fully understood. A comprehensive thesis from the University of Ottawa, examining how people react when confronted with the enormity of the universe, found that cosmic vastness produced genuinely polarized responses. While most participants reported positive experiences—93 percent in one study—a substantial majority also reported negative experiences at 68 percent, including existential distress at 46 percent. When exposed to cosmic vastness, approximately half of the participants reported more elevating experience, and half reported more existential distress.

The variable that predicted which direction a person would go was not intelligence, or education, or prior interest in astronomy. It was self-esteem. The Ottawa research found that self-esteem moderated the relationship between witnessing cosmic vastness and elevating experience, while both self-esteem and meaning in life moderated the relationship between cosmic vastness and existential distress. An independent study published in the *Journal of Experimental Social Psychology*, with the evocative title "A microscopic dot on a microscopic dot," had found that low self-esteem people exposed to the vastness of the universe reported more negative affect, less identification with others, less empathy for victims of a humanitarian disaster, and lower egalitarianism. For high self-esteem individuals, these effects did not emerge.

The multiplanetary man had spent years building the psychological infrastructure that the research identified as protective. He had cultivated a stable sense of self that was not contingent on external validation. He had constructed a meaning framework large enough to accommodate cosmic scale. And yet the fear still arrived. The research did not promise immunity. It promised resilience—the capacity to recover, to integrate, to continue. The fear was not a sign that the preparation had failed. It was a sign that the preparation was being tested at its limit.

What the research had not fully captured, and what the multiplanetary man was learning in the quiet hours of his own confrontation with the void, was that self-esteem and meaning were not static possessions. They were practices. They had to be renewed daily, sometimes hourly, against the constant gravitational pull of cosmic indifference. The same mind that could hold the silence without flinching on Monday could be undone by it on Thursday. The same meaning framework that felt solid in the morning could feel like a thin membrane in the middle of the night, when the darkness outside the window was not a metaphor but a physical presence, and the questions that had seemed manageable in daylight became the only questions that mattered.

