## The Existential Vacuum

Viktor Frankl, whose logotherapy had shaped the multiplanetary man's understanding of responsibility in earlier chapters, had identified a condition that he called the "existential vacuum"—a pervasive sense of meaninglessness that manifested in boredom, apathy, and despair. Frankl believed that the will to meaning was the primary motivational force in human life, and that when this will was frustrated, the result was not merely unhappiness but a kind of existential sickness. The existential vacuum was most acute in moments of leisure, when the distractions of work and social obligation fell away and the individual was left alone with the question of what, if anything, their life was for.

The multiplanetary man had experienced the existential vacuum not in leisure but in the aftermath of the overview effect. The confrontation with cosmic vastness had stripped away the distractions that ordinarily kept the question at bay. The daily routines, the professional obligations, the social performances—all of them had been relativized by the view from orbit. And in the silence that followed, the question had arrived with unusual force: *What is any of this for?*

The question was not new. He had been asking it, in various forms, since childhood. But the context had changed. The childhood question had been animated by curiosity, by the sense that the universe was a puzzle to be solved, a mystery to be explored. The question after the overview effect was animated by something closer to dread. The universe was not a puzzle to be solved. It was an expanse to be endured. The mystery was not whether there was meaning hidden somewhere in the vastness. The mystery was whether meaning could be created at all, in a cosmos that offered no confirmation, no feedback, no response.

Frankl's answer to the existential vacuum had been developed in the most extreme circumstances imaginable—the concentration camps of the Holocaust—and it had a credibility that no academic theory could match. Frankl argued that meaning could be found even in suffering, even in the most degraded and dehumanizing conditions, because meaning was not something that was given by the world. It was something that the individual chose to create. "Everything can be taken from a man," Frankl wrote, "but one thing: the last of the human freedoms—to choose one's attitude in any given set of circumstances, to choose one's own way". The meaning was not in the circumstances. It was in the response to the circumstances.

The multiplanetary man had always found Frankl's insight powerful, but he had also found it somewhat too neat—too easily convertible into a slogan, too readily used to justify suffering that should have been resisted rather than transcended. What he had not fully appreciated, until he confronted the void, was that Frankl's insight was not a solution to the problem of meaninglessness. It was a description of the only viable response to a problem that could not be solved. The cosmos would not provide meaning. The silence would not break. The only option was to create meaning in the face of the silence—not because the creation was guaranteed to succeed, not because the meaning was objectively validated by the universe, but because the alternative was to be paralyzed by the void, and paralysis was a form of death.

