## The Three Dimensions of Smallness

The research on awe had given the multiplanetary man a more precise vocabulary for what he was experiencing. The "small self" hypothesis, which had dominated the literature on awe and self-transcendence, proposed that awe-inspiring experiences reduced self-focus and produced a sense of being part of something larger. This was generally associated with positive outcomes: greater prosociality, greater humility, greater well-being. But a 2021 psychometric study by Tyson and colleagues had revealed that "smallness" was not a single construct. It had three distinct dimensions, and only one of them was unambiguously positive.

The first dimension was the feeling of being in the presence of something vaster than the self. This was the dimension that correlated with positive self-views, with connection, with the sense of belonging to something larger. It was the dimension that the overview effect had activated when the multiplanetary man had looked at Earth and felt himself to be part of a planetary system.

The second dimension was the feeling that day-to-day concerns were trivial in the big picture. This dimension had small negative relationships with self-esteem. It was not destructive, but it was not nourishing either. It was the dimension that activated when the multiplanetary man read the news and felt, with a kind of weary detachment, that the political conflicts and economic anxieties of the day were local turbulences in a vast and indifferent system.

The third dimension was the metaphorical feeling of smallness and insignificance. This dimension had strong negative relationships with self-esteem and moderate negative relationships with feelings of connection to others. It was the dimension that activated when the multiplanetary man looked at the void and felt, not that he was part of something larger, but that he was nothing at all—a temporary arrangement of matter in a cosmos that had no use for him.

The distinction was crucial. The celebratory literature on awe had conflated these dimensions, treating all forms of smallness as if they were variations on the same positive experience. But the research showed that they were psychologically distinct. The first dimension was expansion. The third dimension was erasure. And the multiplanetary man had discovered that the same stimulus—the vastness of the cosmos—could activate either dimension depending on the meaning framework through which it was interpreted.

This was the practical implication of the research: the void did not determine its own meaning. The same infinite silence could be experienced as a liberating expanse or as a crushing emptiness, depending on the cognitive and emotional framework that the observer brought to it. The multiplanetary man could not change the void. He could change the framework. And the change was not a matter of self-deception or positive thinking. It was a matter of choosing which dimension of smallness to cultivate—the dimension that connected him to something larger, rather than the dimension that erased him.

