## The Cosmic Calendar and the Discipline of Meaning

One of the most surprising findings in the research on cosmic vastness was that reminders of the enormity of time could improve well-being rather than damage it. A 2025 study published in the *Journal of Positive Psychology*, led by Matthew Hornsey at the University of Queensland, tested what happened when people were asked to contemplate the cosmic calendar—the distillation of the universe's 13.8-billion-year history into a single year, with human history occupying only the last few seconds of December 31st, and the average human lifespan occupying two-tenths of a second.

The researchers had expected that this exercise might produce anxiety, insignificance, and death anxiety. The results were the opposite. Being reminded of the vastness of time increased forgiveness of others and self-forgiveness. It increased life satisfaction and coping. It decreased anxiety. And the mechanism was not awe, or self-perspective, but the feeling of being small in the context of vastness—specifically, the first dimension of smallness, the sense of being in the presence of something larger, not the third dimension of insignificance.

The finding was counterintuitive, and it suggested something important about the psychology of cosmic confrontation. The void was not inherently terrifying. The terror arose from a particular interpretation of the void—the interpretation that equated smallness with meaninglessness. But the research showed that smallness could also be experienced as liberation. If the universe was vast and human concerns were tiny, then the failures and embarrassments and grudges that loomed so large in daily life were also tiny. The cosmic perspective did not diminish the self. It diminished the self's obsessions. And that diminishment was experienced not as a loss but as a relief.

The multiplanetary man had experienced this relief himself, though he had not known how to articulate it. When he allowed himself to fully feel the scale of cosmic time—to hold in his awareness the 13.8 billion years of the universe's history, the 4.5 billion years of Earth's existence, the 300,000 years of Homo sapiens, the few decades of his own life—the effect was not despair. It was a kind of release. The things that had seemed urgent became less urgent. The things that had seemed important became less important. And what remained, after the release, was a clearer sense of what actually mattered: the quality of his attention, the integrity of his work, the depth of his relationships, the small daily choices that constituted the texture of a life.

The cosmic calendar did not make his life meaningless. It made the meanings that were not his own—the status competitions, the ideological battles, the accumulation of possessions—fall away, leaving only the meanings he had chosen. The void was not the enemy of meaning. It was a filter. It stripped away the counterfeit meanings, the meanings inherited from culture or imposed by social pressure, and left only the meanings that could survive the encounter with infinity.

